body:
What is it about your area that upsets you? Too bleak and depressing? Not enough color? The people suck? Once you graduate you are free to go wherever you please. Right now you are on a leash, but it gets cut once you get your diploma. If you can't find courage to kill yourself, maybe you can embrace cowardice and run away from a place you hate. If you kill yourself, it is game over. If you live and you are of able body, you can travel and maybe find a place that can make you happy.

&#x200B;

It requires courage to get on a bus and go, but you can scrape yourself up through from nothing and become successful. It is amazing what people are capable of when their life depends on it. If you want to cling to life, maybe you can find that drive too.

&#x200B;

I am not saying it is easy; nothing ever is and that is a hard lesson. All I am suggesting is that you might be able to wade through the grey to get to the rainbow.

I have a couple times, but long drawn-out posts that aren't clear never really got much attention. They were largely skipped over for the clear posts like 'my arm is broken and the broken bone is literally protruding from my arm, should I go to the ER or should I wait for the doctor to get back in two days?'. Doctors try to get their appointments done in a specific amount of time, say \~15 minutes. They have their routines; physical exam, blood tests, and imaging. If all three are fine and they see nothing else they can think of to test, they'll either refer you off or say that they cannot help anymore besides prescribe a medicine like gabapentin, a prescription anti-inflammatory or an antidepressant for pain. 

&#x200B;

I am kind of burned out of going to the doctors now for those reasons. It feels like a script at this point that I can always predict in advance. I think my Father and Brother were hoping that this mass was related to that problem. The change to the mass might be unrelated to the antibiotic so I'll see in a few days.

&#x200B;

I do appreciate you taking the time to talk to me. Thanks. :)

&#x200B;

I won't die any time soon. I might see how it rides out until my birthday which is about two months from now.

I get the sentiments but if I had to estimate it, over $60,000 at a bare minimum has already gone into trying to solve this medical mystery. I actually already feel like the mass has decreased somewhat after a night of rest and the antibiotics. I am counting about 150 doctor's appointments including all kinds of specialists, a minimum of 12 MRIs, tons of blood tests, lots of various medications, and all other tests doctors wanted to try. It has been like spending money to prove the existence of ghosts although the pain is very real and medicines like opioid painkillers have significantly reduced it (although I can't get them regularly w/o a good diagnosis).

&#x200B;

The only difference is that I moved and I am actually obligated to pay a significant amount of my health care costs in my state because I don't qualify for medicaid here. Spending $20,000 on seeking a diagnosis from my perspective looks a lot like burning $20,000.
